## 5. Priority tier rationale

### 5.1 P0 — Blocks compliance or financial integrity

These three items are the **minimum required** for Fernly to be a viable payroll system in any regulated jurisdiction. None can be deferred without accepting a material operational or audit risk.

**G1 — Statutory compliance engine.** Without it, payroll produces gross-to-net calculations but no statutory deductions. This is acceptable only if the organisation operates in jurisdictions with zero mandatory payroll deductions — which is rare. The engine must be **DB-driven and super-admin-configurable**, not hard-coded, to support the stated goal of "any company based on its location can configure it accordingly".

**G2 — General ledger.** Without it, payroll totals must be re-keyed into an external accounting system. This creates reconciliation risk on every pay cycle, and makes statutory posting (PF/ESI/TDS payables) impossible. A **minimal double-entry ledger** is sufficient for payroll; full accounting can follow later.

**G8 — Effective-dated salary assignments.** Without it, salary revisions overwrite history. This breaks arrears, breaks statutory tax computation across tax years (which requires monthly salary history), and breaks transfer/promotion records (which reference historical salaries). It is foundational for G1, G6, and G11.

> **Interdependency:** G1, G2, and G8 must be **designed together** in Phase 0, because:
> - G1 needs to reference salary components (which G8 dates).
> - G1's statutory posting needs accounts (which G2 defines).
> - G2's payroll posting needs to reference payslips (which G1 and G8 generate).

### 5.2 P1 — Operational completeness with strong business case

These items unlock real workforce scenarios that Fernly currently cannot support. Each has an independent business case, but they are not existential in the way P0 items are.

**G3 — Full loan management.** Salary advances lite is a deliberate scoping decision. Real loan programmes (housing, vehicle, emergency) require interest, amortization, and multi-year repayment. Separate from advances; different data model.

**G4 — Timesheets & shift management.** Hourly, contractor, and shift-based workforces cannot be paid from recorded time today. This blocks a large class of customers. Timesheet and shift are separable, but typically requested together.

**G5 — Biometric attendance.** Attendance is manually marked; this defeats the purpose of deployed biometric hardware and creates data-quality risk. Depends on the existing `AttendanceRecord` model but needs a device adapter and punch reconciliation.

**G6 — Employee transfer/promotion.** Job history is a compliance and audit requirement in most jurisdictions, and a prerequisite for arrears and lifecycle reporting. **Depends on G8** (effective dating) — this is why G8 is P0 and G6 is P1.

### 5.3 P2 — Extended capability

These items improve the product but are not blockers for core HR/payroll operations.

**G7 — Expense claims.** Reimbursements work today via manual processes outside Fernly. Adding a proper module improves finance integration but is not a payroll compliance requirement.

**G9 — Performance goals / KRAs.** Reviews exist and function. Goal alignment is a maturing capability, not a blocker.

**G10 — Tax declarations.** Employee self-service for tax planning requires G1 to be in place first. Once G1 exists, declarations are a relatively contained addition.

**G11 — Multi-frequency payroll.** Monthly-only payroll is sufficient for many customers. Weekly/fortnightly/off-cycle are valuable but can be deferred until the monthly model is stable and compliant.

### 5.4 P3 — Convenience and polish

These are quality-of-life improvements that do not block operational use.

**G12 — Bank payment files.** CSV exports already exist. Bank-specific formats reduce manual steps but are not critical.

**G13 — Granular onboarding templates.** Current templates work; role-specific templates improve quality but are an optimisation.

**G14 — Mobile app.** The SPA is functional. A mobile app is a convenience improvement, not a payroll blocker.

