## 6. Dependency-ordered sequencing

The matrix encodes dependencies, but the **sequence** in which work is executed matters. The recommended sequence:
Phase 0 — Design (parallel, before any implementation)
├── Statutory meta-model + expression DSL spec
├── Ledger primitive design (accounts, journal entries, posting rules)
└── Effective-dating model (salary assignments, lifecycle documents)

Phase 1 — P0 foundations (in this order)
├── G8 — Effective-dated salary assignments
├── G2 — General ledger (parallel to G8, coordinated on account mapping)
└── G1 — Statutory compliance engine (depends on G8 and G2)

Phase 2 — P1 operational completeness (parallelisable)
├── G5 — Biometric attendance
├── G6 — Employee transfer/promotion (depends on G8)
├── G4 — Timesheet & shift
└── G3 — Full loan management

Phase 3 — P2 extended capability
├── G10 — Tax declarations (depends on G1)
├── G7 — Expense claims (benefits from G2)
├── G9 — Performance goals
└── G11 — Multi-frequency payroll (depends on G1 and G8)

Phase 4 — P3 polish
├── G12 — Bank payment files (depends on G2)
├── G13 — Granular onboarding templates
└── G14 — Mobile app


**Why G8 precedes G1:** statutory tax computation across a tax year requires monthly salary history. Without effective dating, historical salary is not reliably reconstructable.

**Why G2 runs in parallel to G8:** the ledger is independent of payroll data, but account mapping must be agreed before G1 lands, since statutory posting needs accounts.

**Why G1 lands before G10 and G11:** tax declarations and multi-frequency payroll both depend on the statutory engine's period and slab models.

